145
00:19:31.329 --> 00:19:32.389
<v Maya>Right. That's the big question.</v>

146
00:19:33.197 --> 00:19:43.965
<v Daniel>Is the highly complex algebraic method of open AI and the geometric tensor network method of anthropic actually the exact same underlying mathematical idea, just dressed in different clothes?</v>

147
00:19:44.326 --> 00:19:52.952
<v Maya>Did two massive neural networks independently hit the exact same fundamental mathematical truth from two totally different angles in the exact same week?</v>

148
00:19:53.372 --> 00:19:54.073
<v Daniel>We don't know yet.</v>

149
00:19:55.047 --> 00:20:09.480
<v Maya>And on a more philosophical level, for you to ponder on your own, what is a mathematical proof actually worth today if the logic was discovered by AI, translated into code by AI, and formally verified by an AI checker?</v>

150
00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:12.763
<v Maya>At what point does human understanding become secondary?</v>

151
00:20:12.783 --> 00:20:21.871
<v Daniel>Yeah. If the math is flawlessly true, but no human mind is capable of holding the complete architecture of the proof in their head at once, is it still human science?</v>

152
00:20:22.516 --> 00:20:37.264
<v Maya>It's the ultimate locked combination safe. The AI just handed us a piece of paper that says it knows how to open it, and we are holding the combination, but we don't even know if our hands are strong enough to turn the dials. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the bleeding edge of</v>

153
00:20:37.324 --> 00:20:39.725
<v Maya>theoretical computer science. Until next time.</v>
